      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate the inflammatory markers in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) and associations of these markers with obesity and in vitro fertilization (IVF) outcomes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 292 women underwent IVF procedure either with PCOS (n = 146) or without PCOS (n = 146, age, and body mass index (BMI) matched controls) were included in the study. All patients were classified according to BMI levels (normal weight: NW, BMI <25 kg/m(2) and obese: OB, BMI ≥25 kg/m(2)). The inflammatory markers were leukocyte count, neutrophil/lymphocyte ratio (NLR), platelet/lymphocyte ratio (PLR), mean platelet volume (MPV)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "BMI of PCOS was positively correlated with leukocyte, neutrophil, lymphocyte and MPV (p < 0.05), but negatively correlated with NLR and PLR (p < 0.05). Both NLR and PLR increased significantly in PCOS (p < 0.001). PLR increased significantly in NW-PCOS compared the NW-controls and OB-PCOS. MPV values increased only in OB-PCOS subjects. The logistic regression analyzes showed that MPV was the independent variable in PCOS to effect CPR (p = 0.000; OR 0.1; CI 0.06-0.2)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "NLR and PLR were significantly increased in all PCOS subjects compared to the BMI-matched controls. Despite PLR being decreased by adiposity, PLR increased in NW-PCOS. These results are supporting the hypothesis that PCOS is a chronic inflammatory process independent of obesity. MPV levels were independently associated with CPR in PCOS. Further prospective studies concerning inflammation and IVF outcomes of PCOS are needed."
        }
      ]},
